      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
